      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Individual sovereignty argues that the right to speak, sell, or present information is an inalienable property of the individual, not a conditional privilege granted by the government. Against truth-in-advertising, you can argue that the market, through robust competition and consumer choice, is the superior arbiter of truth, making regulatory intervention an unwarranted paternalism. For content moderation, framing it as a government-enforced standard for acceptable thought inherently denies the individual's right to express unconventional or offensive views without state sanction.",
